WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:06.825
Rock isn't all a shot leaves behind.

00:06.825 --> 00:10.225
When explosives detonate, they also give off gases.

00:10.225 --> 00:12.900
The main ones to know are carbon monoxide and the

00:12.900 --> 00:15.725
oxides of nitrogen. Production blasting is the

00:15.725 --> 00:19.000
main source of toxic gas in many underground stone

00:19.000 --> 00:19.913
mines.

00:21.023 --> 00:24.198
Carbon monoxide is colorless and odorless.

00:24.198 --> 00:26.486
You can't see it, and you can't smell it.

00:26.486 --> 00:29.186
The oxides of nitrogen, which include nitrogen

00:29.186 --> 00:32.686
dioxide, can show up as a reddish-brown or orange

00:32.686 --> 00:36.386
cloud, and they can smell sharp. Nitrogen dioxide

00:36.386 --> 00:38.273
is a severe lung irritant.

00:39.390 --> 00:42.027
Don't try to judge fumes with your own senses.

00:42.027 --> 00:45.190
Carbon monoxide has no smell. A clear-looking heading

00:45.190 --> 00:48.690
can still hold gas. And nitrogen dioxide is tricky

00:48.690 --> 00:51.877
because the worst effects may not show up for hours.

00:51.877 --> 00:54.665
A person can walk out feeling okay and be struggling

00:54.665 --> 00:57.127
to breathe that evening. Never sniff to check the

00:57.127 --> 01:00.215
air, and never walk in to see whether it's cleared.

01:03.544 --> 01:06.894
After a shot, the fumes travel with the ventilation.

01:06.894 --> 01:09.106
They move along the current, and they can collect

01:09.106 --> 01:11.819
where the air barely moves, like dead ends and

01:11.819 --> 01:14.569
low spots. Which is why you should never assume

01:14.569 --> 01:17.394
the air outside the blast area is clean just because

01:17.394 --> 01:19.831
you can't see anything. If the airflow has been

01:19.831 --> 01:23.356
changed, by a stopped fan, a damaged curtain,

01:23.356 --> 01:26.131
or an open door, the fumes may go somewhere nobody

01:26.131 --> 01:27.106
expected.

01:28.234 --> 01:31.347
On the surface, the fumes drift with the wind.

01:31.347 --> 01:33.947
The orange cloud you sometimes see after a shot

01:33.947 --> 01:37.109
is a signal to stay away, not a show to watch.

01:37.109 --> 01:39.496
Stay upwind, stay out of the cloud,

01:39.496 --> 01:42.184
and wait for the all-clear. Fumes from a surface

01:42.184 --> 01:45.047
shot can hang in the pit when the air is calm.

01:48.380 --> 01:50.655
How long does it take for the air to clear?

01:50.655 --> 01:53.880
NIOSH measured the gases after blasts in an operating

01:53.880 --> 01:57.017
underground limestone mine. Carbon monoxide took

01:57.017 --> 02:00.168
the longest to clear. The average re-entry time,

02:00.168 --> 02:03.343
based on carbon monoxide, was about one and a third

02:03.343 --> 02:06.593
hours. At one standard deviation, it was about

02:06.593 --> 02:09.792
two and three-quarter hours. And on some shots,

02:09.792 --> 02:11.543
it was well over three hours.

02:12.675 --> 02:15.912
That's one mine, not ours. We show it for one reason.

02:15.912 --> 02:18.075
A clock on the wall is not a clearance,

02:18.075 --> 02:20.662
and a shot is not safe to enter because "it's been

02:20.662 --> 02:21.550
a while."

02:22.784 --> 02:25.659
Re-entry takes three things. The ventilation has

02:25.659 --> 02:28.172
to have run long enough to clear the area.

02:28.172 --> 02:30.821
The air has to be tested with the right instrument.

02:30.821 --> 02:33.159
And an authorized supervisor has to release the

02:33.159 --> 02:37.246
area. Supervisors, not trainees, carry the ALTAIR

02:37.246 --> 02:40.772
four-X gas monitor at our sites. Your site's minimum

02:40.772 --> 02:43.759
wait and the person who releases the area are covered

02:43.759 --> 02:45.596
in your site-specific training.

02:46.730 --> 02:49.317
And remember what the four-gas monitor reads.

02:49.317 --> 02:52.843
Oxygen, flammable gas, carbon monoxide and hydrogen

02:52.843 --> 02:56.080
sulfide. It doesn't read nitrogen dioxide.

02:56.080 --> 02:58.942
A quiet monitor doesn't prove the air is safe from

02:58.942 --> 03:01.980
every gas, which is why the wait, the testing,

03:01.980 --> 03:04.405
and the supervisor's say-so all count.

03:05.516 --> 03:08.841
A word on your W65. It protects against carbon

03:08.841 --> 03:12.216
monoxide only. It isn't approved for low oxygen

03:12.216 --> 03:15.353
or for other toxic gases, which includes the nitrogen

03:15.353 --> 03:19.328
oxides in blast fumes. So the W65 is not a way

03:19.328 --> 03:22.741
to walk into fresh blast fumes. We cover its limits

03:22.741 --> 03:24.091
in Module 3.

03:25.227 --> 03:27.602
The blocks and warning signs that closed off the

03:27.602 --> 03:30.952
area before the shot stay in place until an authorized

03:30.952 --> 03:33.714
supervisor releases it. Don't move one,

03:33.714 --> 03:36.489
step around one, or take one down because you think

03:36.489 --> 03:38.952
the air has had enough time. If you need to get

03:38.952 --> 03:41.927
through, ask your supervisor. If somebody tells

03:41.927 --> 03:44.852
you it's fine and the supervisor hasn't said so,

03:44.852 --> 03:46.352
the answer is still no.

03:47.493 --> 03:50.042
The wait is part of the job, not a delay to be

03:50.042 --> 03:53.292
beaten. Expect it, and use it. There's always other

03:53.292 --> 03:56.142
work, away from the shot area. The temptation to

03:56.142 --> 03:59.030
shave a little time off the wait is real,

03:59.030 --> 04:01.368
and that's exactly why the rule exists,

04:01.368 --> 04:04.355
and why a supervisor, not the crew's impatience,

04:04.355 --> 04:06.155
decides when to go back in.

04:09.487 --> 04:13.024
Burning eyes or throat, coughing, chest tightness,

04:13.024 --> 04:15.786
a headache, or dizziness after being around blast

04:15.786 --> 04:18.749
fumes are warning signs. Get to fresh air right

04:18.749 --> 04:22.674
away, tell your supervisor, and get a medical evaluation,

04:22.674 --> 04:25.099
even if you start to feel better. With nitrogen

04:25.099 --> 04:28.012
dioxide, serious effects can be delayed,

04:28.012 --> 04:30.799
so feeling fine doesn't mean you are fine.

04:31.945 --> 04:34.532
If someone else is affected, get help and send

04:34.532 --> 04:37.157
for medical care. Don't go into the bad air after

04:37.157 --> 04:39.932
them. A second person down helps nobody.

04:41.071 --> 04:43.334
Here's what that looks like on a real shift.

04:43.334 --> 04:46.146
The area was shot an hour ago. A coworker says

04:46.146 --> 04:48.709
it looks clear, and that you could save time by

04:48.709 --> 04:51.384
getting the equipment in now. You say no.

04:51.384 --> 04:53.884
You can't see carbon monoxide, and the monitor

04:53.884 --> 04:57.459
on a supervisor's belt doesn't read nitrogen dioxide.

04:57.459 --> 04:59.321
You say you'll wait for the release.

04:59.321 --> 05:01.684
You get on the radio and let the supervisor know

05:01.684 --> 05:04.409
people are asking about going in. That's what stopping

05:04.409 --> 05:06.009
unsafe work looks like.

05:07.141 --> 05:10.016
To sum up. Blasting leaves carbon monoxide and

05:10.016 --> 05:12.791
nitrogen oxides behind. You can't judge them by

05:12.791 --> 05:15.541
sight or smell. They follow the air.

05:15.541 --> 05:18.128
Re-entry happens only after the ventilation has

05:18.128 --> 05:21.115
run, the air has been tested, and an authorized

05:21.115 --> 05:25.203
supervisor releases the area. Your W65 is for carbon

05:25.203 --> 05:27.953
monoxide only. And if you have symptoms,

05:27.953 --> 05:30.378
get to fresh air, tell your supervisor,

05:30.378 --> 05:31.466
and get checked.

05:32.586 --> 05:35.561
Your site's plans always override this video.
